from _lib import *

NET1 = module("NET1", "Networks 1: what a network is - addresses, ports, names and the envelope", "Networks",
  "You fixed DNS before you knew what it was. Now you learn the whole post office: how a message from your laptop finds a computer across the world, which door it knocks on, how the name became a number, and why the letter is sealed. Every security idea after this stands on these four.",
  "the family", "A one-page 'how the home Wi-Fi works' sheet for the family: the router, the addresses, the phone book, and the one rule (change the default password) - which she then does with Abid.",
  [
    lesson("NET1-L1", "Addresses: IP, the home network, and the two kinds of address",
      ["Find her laptop's IP address and the router's, and explain private vs public addresses", "Explain what a subnet is with the house-and-street picture", "Use ping to prove two machines on the home network can reach each other"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "THE PROBLEM: millions of computers; how does a message find one? THE MECHANISM: every machine on a network has a number (IP address, four numbers like 192.168.0.12); the home has a private street of numbers behind the router, and the router has one public number the world sees (draw: house numbers inside, one front door). HER HANDS: ip a (her address; read the 192.168.x.x line); ip route (the router = default gateway); ping -c 3 <router> (round-trip time - milliseconds, define); ping -c 3 1.1.1.1 (a public address; it answers); a phone on the same Wi-Fi: its address from its settings; ping it (if it answers). Private ranges (192.168., 10., 172.16-31.) - she says why two houses can both have a '192.168.0.12' (different streets). Check: private vs public, and what the gateway is."),
    lesson("NET1-L2", "Ports: one address, many doors",
      ["Explain a port as a numbered door on one machine and name five common ones (22, 53, 80, 443, 25)", "See her laptop's open ports with ss and explain what 'listening' means", "Explain why an open door is a risk only if something weak is behind it - and why defenders close the ones they do not need"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "One address, many programs: the web server, ssh, e-mail - each waits at a numbered door. HER HANDS: ss -tuln (read: a listening socket - tcp/udp, address:port); on her Mint laptop there are few; python3 -m http.server 8000 in ~/school (a web server in one line) then ss -tuln again (8000 appears) then in Firefox http://localhost:8000 (her own files, served by her own program) then Ctrl+C. Curl: curl -I http://localhost:8000. The defender's rule: every open port is a door you must be able to name and defend; ss -tuln on a server is the first thing checked (DEF1). Check: five ports and their programs; 'listening' in her words."),
    lesson("NET1-L3", "Names: DNS, the phone book she already fixed",
      ["Explain the DNS chain (her laptop -> resolver -> root -> .com -> the site) with a drawing", "Use dig / nslookup to look up a name and read the answer, TTL included", "Explain why the ISP's broken resolver stopped Windows but not Chrome (CS1B-L2) in network words"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "She lived this. Now the mechanism: a name is for humans; the network needs the number; a resolver asks up the chain until someone knows; the answer is cached for TTL seconds. HER HANDS: dig the-school-orpin.vercel.app (read ANSWER, the A record, the TTL); dig +short google.com; dig @1.1.1.1 example.com (ask a different phone book); cat /etc/resolv.conf (which resolver her laptop uses - NextDNS via the DoH setting from LX1-L7; explain DoH: the question goes sealed so nobody on the way can read or change it); the NextDNS test page button. Replay CS1B: Windows asked the ISP's book, which answered nothing for Microsoft; Chrome carried its own. Security: a lying phone book sends you to a fake bank - which is why names are checked by certificates (L4). Check: resolver, record, TTL."),
    lesson("NET1-L4", "The envelope: HTTP, TLS, and what the padlock proves",
      ["Explain a request and a response (method, path, status code, headers, body) by reading real ones with curl -v", "Explain TLS as a sealed envelope plus an identity card (the certificate) and what the padlock does and does not prove", "Read a certificate in Firefox and say who issued it and until when"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "HER HANDS: curl -v http://example.com (read the request lines and the 200 OK; a 404 on a wrong path; headers as labels on the envelope); curl -v https://the-school-orpin.vercel.app/api/ping (the TLS handshake lines: the certificate, the issuer); Firefox padlock -> certificate -> issued by, valid until. Mechanism in two pictures: the envelope (encryption: nobody on the path reads it) and the ID card (the certificate: this server is who it says, vouched for by an issuer the browser trusts). What the padlock does NOT prove: that the site is honest - a scam can have a padlock; it proves only that you are talking to the name in the bar. Status codes: 200, 301, 404, 500 - she has met 500 (the crash page). Check: request vs response; what the padlock proves and does not."),
    code("NET1-CODE1", "Code: Python asks a server a question",
      ["Make an HTTP request from Python and read the status code and body", "Handle the two failures (no network, bad status) with a kind message", "Say what the program would need to send a request through TLS and why it is the default"],
      "# A tiny client. In the browser editor this cannot reach the network,\n# so we practise the shape; in your terminal the real one runs.\nimport json\n\nresponse_status = 200\nresponse_body = '{\"ok\": true, \"at\": \"2026-10-20T10:00:00Z\"}'\n\nif response_status == 200:\n    data = json.loads(response_body)\n    print(\"the server says ok:\", data[\"ok\"])\nelse:\n    print(\"the server answered\", response_status)\n",
      "Then the real thing in her terminal: python3 with urllib.request: urlopen('https://the-school-orpin.vercel.app/api/ping') -> status, read(), json.loads. try/except URLError for no network; check status != 200. She prints the 'at' time and compares it to date. Boundary Question: what if the body is not JSON? (json.loads raises - catch it). Save: her first network program."),
    lesson("NET1-L5", "The router: the one box the whole house trusts",
      ["Log in to the home router's page (with Abid), read the connected devices and the DHCP table", "Explain DHCP (addresses handed out), NAT (one public door for all), and the firewall it already runs", "Change the router's default admin password (Abid types it; it goes in his vault) and explain why that is the single most important fix in most homes"],
      FROM_ZERO + LAB + "With Abid on a call: the router's page (its address = the gateway from L1); the device list (every phone and the laptop - she names each); the DHCP table (who got which number); NAT explained with the front-door picture; the firewall tab (the default: nothing from outside may knock on inside doors). THE FIX: the admin password is often 'admin' and printed on the box - change it; Abid types it (the tutor never sees any password). Optional: set the router's DNS to NextDNS so every device in the house gets the filtered phone book (the 30-day-streak project does this fully). THE GIFT comes from this lesson. Check: DHCP, NAT, firewall - one line each."),
    lesson("NET1-H1", "Horizon: the internet is cables, deals and chokepoints",
      ["Explain that the internet is many networks that agree to connect (and what an ISP, an exchange and an undersea cable are)", "Give the mechanism of a country going dark (cables, orders to ISPs, DNS) and of a country filtering (NET-level, DNS-level, inspection)", "Ask which part protects and which controls, with two examples"],
      HORIZON + "Mechanism: ISPs connect at exchanges; continents connect by undersea cables (most of Bangladesh's traffic goes through a few landing points - one example among many countries with the same shape); traceroute to a far server shows the hops (she runs it: traceroute 1.1.1.1 or mtr). A cable cut or an order to ISPs can isolate a country - several have happened; a filter can sit at DNS (she knows), at addresses, or by inspection. The same inspection catches scams and reads citizens' mail. No how-to for evasion (CURIOSITY RULE). Boundary Question: 'You own the only cable into an island. Who checks what you do with it?'"),
    check("NET1-C", "Networks 1 checkpoint", ["ip-addresses-private-public", "ports-and-listening", "dns-chain", "http-and-tls", "router-dhcp-nat"], count=10),
    teach("NET1-T", "Explain to a 10-year-old how a message from your laptop reaches a website: the address, the door, the name and the sealed envelope. Then say what the padlock does not prove. Say it out loud first, then write it. 5-7 sentences."),
  ],
  read={"url": "https://www.cloudflare.com/learning/dns/what-is-dns/", "title": "Read: what is DNS - a clear explanation from a company that runs a resolver"})

NET2 = module("NET2", "Networks 2: her Raspberry Pi as a server - the lab box", "Networks",
  "A server is a computer that waits for others to ask it things. Yours is the size of a bank card, sits on your desk, and is the first machine in your lab: you will set it up from nothing, reach it from your laptop without a screen, serve a page from it, and keep it safe. Everything you learn about defending machines, you will practise here first.",
  "Abid", "A page served from her own Pi on the home network: 'Faiza's lab - day 1', with uptime and a photo, shown to Abid on the call from her laptop's browser.",
  [
    lesson("NET2-L1", "First boot: the OS on a card, and a machine with no screen",
      ["Write Raspberry Pi OS Lite to the SD card with the official imager, setting hostname, user, password and SSH before first boot", "Explain why a server runs 'Lite' (no desktop) and what 'headless' means", "Find the Pi on the network and ssh into it from her laptop"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "The Pi reward is in hand (CS3). Imager (button) on her Mint laptop: OS = Raspberry Pi OS Lite (64-bit); the settings gear: hostname faiza-pi, username hers, a generated password into Bitwarden, enable SSH with password for now (keys in L2), Wi-Fi name and password typed by her. Why Lite: a server has no one looking at it; a desktop is wasted memory. Boot: card in, power on, wait a minute; from her laptop: ping faiza-pi.local (mDNS - a name without DNS, explain); ssh <user>@faiza-pi.local; the first login; hostnamectl; uname -a (it is Linux - the same system as her laptop, on a different chip - ARM, define). Check: headless, hostname, what ssh gave her.",
      links=[link("Raspberry Pi Imager - download", "https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/", "the official tool that writes the OS to the SD card", file="imager_*_amd64.deb or the AppImage")]),
    lesson("NET2-L2", "Keys, not passwords: ssh done properly, and the first hardening",
      ["Copy her SSH public key to the Pi and turn off password login; explain why", "Update the Pi, set the timezone, and create the admin/daily split she has on her laptop", "Explain what 'attack surface' means and list the Pi's (one open port)"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "Her laptop already has a key (CS5-L3). HER HANDS: ssh-copy-id <user>@faiza-pi.local; ssh in without a password; then on the Pi: sudo nano /etc/ssh/sshd_config -> PasswordAuthentication no; sudo systemctl restart ssh; from a second terminal test the key login BEFORE closing the first (the rule from the school's own standing rules: never lock the old door until the new one is proved). sudo apt update && sudo apt full-upgrade; sudo timedatectl set-timezone Asia/Dhaka; ss -tuln (one door: 22). Attack surface (define: every way in) = that one door, now key-only. Check: why keys beat passwords (nothing to guess, nothing reused, nothing to leak)."),
    lesson("NET2-L3", "Serve a page: a web server she runs, reads the logs of, and understands",
      ["Install nginx, serve a page she wrote, and reach it from her laptop and phone", "Read the access log and explain each field of one line (who, when, what, status)", "Explain static vs dynamic and where her Python app will go (WEB1)"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "sudo apt install nginx; from the laptop http://faiza-pi.local (the welcome page); sudo nano /var/www/html/index.html -> her page: 'Faiza's lab - day 1' plus the date; reload; sudo tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log while she loads the page from her phone: read the line - the phone's address, the time, GET / HTTP/1.1, 200, the browser's name. Then a wrong path from the phone -> 404 in the log. This log is what defenders read first (DEF1). Static (a file handed over) vs dynamic (a program answers - WEB1). Check: one log line, field by field."),
    lesson("NET2-L4", "The firewall: a list of doors, and everything else closed",
      ["Install and enable ufw with deny-incoming default, allow 22 and 80, and explain each rule", "Test from the laptop that a closed port is closed (nc / nmap on her OWN Pi only)", "Explain why a firewall on the box matters even behind the router's"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "sudo apt install ufw; sudo ufw default deny incoming; sudo ufw default allow outgoing; sudo ufw allow 22; sudo ufw allow 80; sudo ufw enable; sudo ufw status verbose. From the laptop, a port scan OF HER OWN PI ONLY (she says the lab rule first): nmap faiza-pi.local (install nmap on the laptop) - 22 and 80 open, nothing else; nc -zv faiza-pi.local 8000 -> refused. Why two firewalls: the router stops the outside; the Pi's stops a compromised phone on the same Wi-Fi. Boundary Question: 'You forgot ufw allow 22 before enable - what happens, and how do you get back in?' (the screen and keyboard, or the SD card on the laptop). Check: the three rules in her words."),
    lesson("NET2-L5", "Watch it: logs, logins, and the habit of looking",
      ["Read auth.log / journalctl for ssh logins and failed attempts, and explain what a brute-force attempt looks like in a log", "Install fail2ban and explain, as mechanism, what it does", "Write the Pi's weekly check into a script and a cron line (uptime, disk, updates pending, last logins)"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "sudo journalctl -u ssh | tail (her own logins, with the key); last (who logged in when); she makes a failed attempt from the laptop with a wrong user and finds it in the log - what a thousand of those in a minute would mean. fail2ban (define: watches the log, bans an address after N failures): install, status, and why it is a defence that uses the attacker's own behaviour. The check script (CS3-L5 shape) with a cron line (CS4-L3) that appends to ~/pi-check.log weekly. Check: what a brute-force attempt looks like, and what fail2ban reads."),
    lesson("NET2-H1", "Horizon: every server on earth is this, times a million",
      ["Explain what a data centre is and why servers are headless, logged and firewalled by default", "Give one story of a server left with a default password and what defenders learned (change defaults, scan your own doors)", "Ask who benefits, how would you break it, how would you fix it - for her own Pi"],
      HORIZON + "Her Pi is the smallest honest model of a bank's server room: no screen, keys only, a firewall, logs, a schedule. Stories as mechanism: internet-wide scans find devices with default passwords within minutes of going online (cameras, routers) - that is why L2 and NET1-L5 changed defaults first. Defenders' habits: change defaults, close doors, read logs, update. Boundary Question for her Pi: 'Which of your doors would you attack first, and what would stop you?' (her own answer goes in the logbook - it is her first threat model)."),
    check("NET2-C", "Networks 2 checkpoint", ["headless-and-ssh", "keys-and-hardening", "web-server-and-logs", "firewall-rules", "logs-and-fail2ban"], count=10),
    teach("NET2-T", "Explain to a 10-year-old what a server is and how you reach yours without a screen, then explain to Abid the three things you did to keep it safe and why each one works. Say it out loud first, then write it. 5-7 sentences."),
  ],
  read={"url": "https://www.raspberrypi.com/documentation/computers/getting-started.html", "title": "Read: Raspberry Pi - getting started, from the makers"})

DEF1 = module("DEF1", "Defence 1: keeping a machine safe - patch, least privilege, backup, watch", "Security",
  "Attackers need one open door. Defenders must know every door, close the ones not needed, and watch the ones that are. This module is the defender's checklist, done for real on your Pi and your laptop - and it is most of what a security team does every day.",
  "the family", "A one-page 'home security checklist' she writes for the family's phones and laptops (updates on, screen lock, unique passwords, backups), checked with each person on a call.",
  [
    lesson("DEF1-L1", "Why updates: the bug, the fix, the race",
      ["Explain a vulnerability (a bug an attacker can use) and a patch (the fix) and why the time between them is a race", "Read what apt wants to update and find one security fix in the changelog", "Set up unattended security updates on the Pi and explain the trade-off"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "CS1B-S1's idea, now with hands. Mechanism: software has bugs; some let a stranger do things; when one is found, the maker ships a fix; the day the fix is public, attackers read it too - every unpatched machine is now a known door. HER HANDS: sudo apt update; apt list --upgradable; apt changelog <one package> (find the word 'security' or a CVE number - define CVE: a numbered public record of a vulnerability); sudo apt full-upgrade. On the Pi: sudo apt install unattended-upgrades; the config; the trade-off (a server that updates itself may restart a service - acceptable for her lab). Check: vulnerability, patch, CVE, the race."),
    lesson("DEF1-L2", "Least privilege, again, everywhere: users, services, sudo, and what runs as root",
      ["List what runs as root on the Pi (ps -u root) and say which could run as less", "Create a service user for the web page and explain why nginx workers already drop privileges", "Audit her own sudo use: what she typed with sudo this week and whether each needed it"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "CS3-L2 as a habit. ps -eo user,pid,cmd | sort | head; nginx's master is root, its workers are www-data - she reads it and says why (bind port 80 needs root; serving files does not). A deliberately bad idea shown, not done: running a web app as root means a bug in the app is a bug with every right. sudo journalctl _COMM=sudo | tail (her sudo history) - each line: needed, or habit? Boundary Question: 'An app you run as root has a file-upload bug. What can the attacker now read?' (everything). Check: least privilege in one sentence of her own."),
    lesson("DEF1-L3", "Passwords and keys: the mechanism behind every rule she follows",
      ["Explain hashing (one-way) and why a leaked hash is still a problem (guessing) - with a Python demo", "Explain why length beats cleverness and why reuse is the real disaster (a leak at one site opens the others)", "Check an e-mail address against a breach-notification service and set the alert"],
      FROM_ZERO + LAB + "Mechanism: a site stores a hash (define: a one-way scramble; same input, same output; no way back) of her password, not the password; Python: hashlib.sha256(b'...').hexdigest() - she changes one letter and sees the whole thing change. A leaked hash list is attacked by guessing billions of common passwords and hashing each - so short or common passwords fall in seconds; a 20-character generated one does not. Reuse: one leak, every account. Her habits (Bitwarden, 2FAS, the master-password protocol) now have their reasons. The breach-notification site (button): her Proton address; subscribe for alerts; Abid's too, with his yes. Check: hash, guessing, reuse - and the one password she remembers.",
      links=[link("Have I Been Pwned", "https://haveibeenpwned.com/", "checks whether an e-mail address appears in known data leaks; free, run by a well-known security researcher")]),
    lesson("DEF1-L4", "Backups as defence: the 3-2-1 rule and the restore drill",
      ["State 3-2-1 (three copies, two kinds of place, one away from home) and map her own copies onto it", "Back up the Pi's config and web page to the SSD with rsync; restore one file", "Explain why ransomware fails against a good backup, as mechanism"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "Her copies: laptop, the encrypted SSD (CS1C-L4), GitHub for the archive (CS5) - that is 3-2-1 already for the school folder; she says which is which. The Pi: rsync -av from the Pi (over ssh: rsync -av <user>@faiza-pi.local:/etc/nginx ~/pi-backup/) and /var/www; restore the index page after deleting it on purpose. Ransomware mechanism: encrypt the victim's files, sell the key - useless if yesterday's copy is on a drive that was not plugged in. Hence the ritual: plug in, back up, EJECT. Check: 3-2-1 for her own files."),
    lesson("DEF1-L5", "Watch: a baseline, a diff, and the five questions for a strange machine",
      ["Record a baseline of the Pi (open ports, users, cron, services, listening programs) in a file and commit it", "Re-run the baseline a week later and diff it; explain what a surprise would mean", "Answer the five questions on her own Pi: what listens, who can log in, what runs on a schedule, what changed, what do the logs say"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "A defender's first move on any machine is to write down what normal looks like. Script baseline.sh: ss -tuln; cat /etc/passwd | cut -d: -f1; crontab -l and ls /etc/cron*; systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running; ps aux | head -40 - all into baseline-<date>.txt; commit it to a private repo or keep it on the SSD. A week later: diff. The five questions on an index card above her desk. Then the planted surprise: the tutor tells her to add a harmless cron line on the Pi 'as if an attacker had' and, next day, to find it with the diff - she does, and removes it. Check: the five questions from memory."),
    lesson("DEF1-H1", "Horizon: defenders in the world - CERTs, bounties, and the honest trade",
      ["Explain what a CERT does, what a bug bounty is, and what responsible disclosure means", "Give the mechanism of one large breach that a basic checklist would have stopped, and what changed after", "Say what she would do if she found a real bug in someone else's system (stop, write it down, tell an adult, report properly)"],
      HORIZON + "Countries and companies keep teams whose whole job is this checklist at scale; researchers who find bugs report them and are paid (bounties) or thanked - never exploit them. One breach as mechanism (an unpatched server and a known vulnerability, months after the patch existed) - name no villains, explain the chain. Her rule, said in her words: find a bug -> stop -> write it down -> tell Abid -> report it to the owner the proper way. Boundary Question: 'A friend's school website has an obvious bug. What do you do, and what do you never do?'"),
    check("DEF1-C", "Defence 1 checkpoint", ["vulnerability-patch-cve", "least-privilege-practice", "hashing-and-reuse", "backup-3-2-1", "baseline-and-diff"], count=10),
    teach("DEF1-T", "Explain to the family, in plain words, the four things that keep a machine safe - updates, least privilege, backups, watching - with one example of each you did on your own Pi. Say it out loud first, then write it. 5-7 sentences."),
  ])

M12 = module("M12", "Mathematics 12: Algebra II - two unknowns, graphs, and the line that models the world", "Mathematics",
  "One unknown is a box. Two unknowns are a picture: every pair that fits an equation is a point, and the points make a line. Lines are how engineers model almost everything - a cost, a speed, a running time - and crossing two lines is how two facts become one answer.",
  "Abid", "A Python program that draws a graph of Abid's monthly internet cost against speed for the providers she compared (the 30-day-streak project), with the best-value line marked.",
  [
    lesson("M12-L1", "Coordinates: every pair is a point, every rule is a picture",
      ["Plot points from a table and read points from a picture (x across, y up, negatives too)", "Make a table for y = 2x + 1 and plot it; say why it is a line", "Explain what the slope and the intercept mean in a story (cost per item, starting fee)"],
      FROM_ZERO + "Draw the axes (one small SVG). Points: (3, 2) means 3 across, 2 up; negatives go left and down. Table for y = 2x + 1: x = -2..3; plot; a straight line. Slope (define: up per across) = 2; intercept = where it crosses the y line = 1. Story: a taxi charges 1 to start and 2 per km - the same line. Six points to plot, two lines to table. Check: slope and intercept of y = 3x - 2 in the taxi story."),
    practice("M12-P1", "Practice: points and lines", ["plot-and-read-points", "table-for-a-line", "slope-and-intercept"]),
    code("M12-CODE1", "Code: Python draws the line",
      ["Compute a table of (x, y) for y = mx + c in a loop and print it", "Draw the points as text (a simple grid of characters) and see the slope", "Change m and c and predict the picture before running"],
      "# A line as a table, then as a rough picture.\nm, c = 2, 1\nfor x in range(0, 6):\n    y = m * x + c\n    print(x, y, \"*\" * y)\n",
      "The stars make a rough graph - the slope is visible. She changes m to 1, 3, 0 and -1 (negative: the picture needs care - she handles y < 0 with a guard). Then, in her terminal, real plotting: python3 -m pip install matplotlib (explain pip is Python's apt) and five lines to draw y = 2x + 1 as a true graph in a window. The gift's shape starts here. Save."),
    lesson("M12-L2", "Two unknowns, two facts: where the lines cross",
      ["Set up two equations from a story (two prices, two totals) and solve by substitution", "Solve the same pair by elimination and say when each is easier", "Check by putting both answers back into both equations"],
      FROM_ZERO + "Story: 2 samosas and 1 tea cost 50; 1 samosa and 1 tea cost 35. Two boxes, two facts. Substitution: from the second, t = 35 - s; put it in the first: 2s + 35 - s = 50 -> s = 15, t = 20. Elimination: subtract the second from the first: s = 15. Picture: each equation is a line; the answer is where they cross (plot both). Check both. Four pairs by hand, Python check for each. Boundary Question: two parallel lines (2s + 2t = 10 and s + t = 7) - no crossing: what does that mean, and what does Python's solver say?"),
    practice("M12-P2", "Practice: two unknowns", ["substitution", "elimination", "story-to-equations"]),
    lesson("M12-L3", "Inequalities and the shape of 'at most': budgets, limits, and shading",
      ["Solve 3x + 5 < 20 and explain the one rule that differs from equations (flipping on a negative)", "Draw an inequality on a number line and a two-variable one as a shaded region", "Model a budget: at most 500 taka, samosas 15 and teas 20 - which pairs are allowed?"],
      FROM_ZERO + "Equations say 'exactly'; the world mostly says 'at most'. Solve as before; the single trap: multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative flips the sign - show why with -x < 3. Number line with an open or filled circle. The budget as a shaded region under a line - she lists five allowed pairs and two not allowed, then Python checks any pair with one if. Check: solve 10 - 2x >= 4."),
    practice("M12-P3", "Practice: inequalities", ["solve-inequality", "flip-on-negative", "budget-region"]),
    lesson("M12-H1", "Horizon: the line that runs the world - models, forecasts and their limits",
      ["Explain what a model is (a simple rule standing in for a messy thing) and why lines are the first model engineers try", "Give the mechanism of a wrong forecast (a line extended past where it was measured)", "Connect to her own pace line on the rewards page (XP per day -> ETA) and say when it lies"],
      HORIZON + "Her rewards page draws a line through her last 14 days and extends it: that is a model, and it is wrong whenever her days change shape (exams, vacation). Models in the world: cost per user, traffic per hour, growth per year - all lines first, then curves. Mechanism of failure: extrapolation past the data; a line fitted to a short, unusual period. Boundary Question: 'Your pace line says the Pi arrives in 12 days. Name two things that would make it wrong, and one that would make it right sooner.'"),
    check("M12-C", "Mathematics 12 checkpoint", ["plot-and-read-points", "slope-and-intercept", "substitution", "elimination", "solve-inequality"], count=12),
    teach("M12-T", "Explain to a younger student what a graph of a line shows, what the slope means in a real story, and how two lines crossing gives an answer to a two-unknown problem. Say it out loud first, then write it. 4-6 sentences."),
  ])

SS2 = module("SS2", "Systems & Society 2: money and banks - the ledger behind everything", "Systems & Society",
  "Money is not paper. It is a ledger: a record of who owes what to whom, and the trust that the record is kept honestly. Once you see money as a ledger, banks, cards, inflation, mobile money and even cryptocurrencies become the same machine with different keepers.",
  "Abid", "A one-page explanation, drawn by her, of what happens inside the banks when Abid sends money from Canada to Dhaka - every hop, every fee, every day of delay - to show him on a call.",
  [
    lesson("SS2-L1", "Money is a ledger: what it is for and who keeps the record",
      ["Explain money's three jobs (measure, exchange, store) and why a ledger does all three", "Trace a payment as entries in two ledgers (hers down, the shop's up) with no paper moving", "Explain why trust in the keeper is the whole thing"],
      FROM_ZERO + "Mechanism, not opinion. Start from barter's problem (the double coincidence of wants), then a token everyone accepts, then the realisation that the token is just a way of writing down 'who is owed'. A bank account IS a line in a ledger. Draw two ledgers; a card payment moves numbers, not paper. The keeper (a bank, a central bank, a mobile-money company, a blockchain's many computers) is trusted to not write false lines. Check: three jobs, and what a payment really moves."),
    lesson("SS2-L2", "Banks: where money is made (really), and what a central bank does",
      ["Explain how a loan creates a deposit (bank money) with a two-ledger drawing", "Explain reserves, a bank run, and why deposit insurance exists, as mechanism", "Explain the central bank's two levers (the interest rate, the amount of money) and what each does to prices"],
      FROM_ZERO + "The surprising mechanism: when a bank lends 100, it writes +100 in the borrower's account - new money, matched by the borrower's debt. Most money is this. A run: everyone asks for paper at once; the bank has lent most of it; insurance and the central bank stand behind it. The central bank: sets the price of borrowing (the rate) - higher rate, less borrowing, cooler prices; it can also create money itself. Examples from several countries, including Bangladesh's central bank as one. Check: how a loan makes money; what a run is."),
    lesson("SS2-L3", "Prices, inflation and exchange rates: why her taka buys less, and what a rate is",
      ["Explain inflation as more money chasing the same goods, measured by a basket, and why it hurts savers", "Explain an exchange rate as a price like any other, set by who wants what", "Compute what 1,000 taka of savings is worth after 3 years at 8% inflation (M8 percentages) in Python"],
      FROM_ZERO + "The basket: the same list of goods priced every month; the change is inflation. Causes as mechanism: more money, fewer goods, higher costs. Who loses: savers in cash; who gains: borrowers (the debt shrinks in real terms). Exchange rate: taka per dollar is a price; it moves with trade, remittances (Abid's), interest rates, confidence. Python: 1000 / (1.08 ** 3). Check: basket, who loses from inflation, what moves a rate."),
    lesson("SS2-L4", "Moving money: cards, mobile money, remittances, and where the fees hide",
      ["Trace a card payment through the five parties (customer, card network, two banks, shop) and the fee at each", "Trace Abid's remittance (bank -> correspondent -> local bank, or a transfer app) with days and fees", "Explain mobile money (bKash as one example among many in the world) as a ledger with phones as the branch"],
      FROM_ZERO + "THE GIFT: the remittance drawing. Mechanism: banks do not all hold accounts with each other; a 'correspondent' bank in the middle does - each hop is a day and a fee; newer transfer apps hold money in both countries and just update two ledgers (faster, cheaper, same trust question). Mobile money: a ledger kept by a phone company; the agent at the corner is the branch - M-Pesa in Kenya and bKash in Bangladesh as two examples of one idea. Cards: the five parties and the percentage. Check: why a remittance takes days; who takes a fee."),
    lesson("SS2-H1", "Horizon: trust, fraud and the ledger - from counterfeits to cryptocurrencies",
      ["Explain the two ways a ledger fails (a false line, a stolen key) and how each is defended", "Explain a cryptocurrency as a ledger kept by many strangers with maths instead of a bank, and its honest trade-offs (no keeper to call, keys that cannot be reset, speculation)", "Ask who benefits, how would you break it, how would you fix it - for a bank and for a blockchain"],
      HORIZON + "Mechanism only, no advice, no opinions on investing (and the tutor says so if asked - she is 13 and this is not financial advice). Fraud = a false line (an insider, a forged instruction) or a stolen key (a card number, a password, a phishing page - the same mechanism as DEF1-L3). Defences: two people for big moves, logs, alerts, the breach-check habit. Blockchain as mechanism: a ledger copied to many computers that agree by rules; strengths and costs honestly. Boundary Question: 'If you lose the key to a bank account you call the bank; if you lose the key to a blockchain wallet, what happens - and why is that both a feature and a danger?'"),
    check("SS2-C", "Systems & Society 2 checkpoint", ["money-as-ledger", "bank-money-and-runs", "inflation-and-rates", "payments-and-remittances"], count=10),
    teach("SS2-T", "Explain to a 10-year-old what money really is, using the word ledger, and then explain to Abid what happens when he sends money home and where the days and fees go. Say it out loud first, then write it. 5-7 sentences."),
  ])

